  "question": {
    "text": "For the function\n$f(x)=\\sin x+3 x-\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\left(x^2+x\\right), \\text { where } x \\in\\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right],$\nconsider the following two statements :\n(I) $\\mathrm{f}$ is increasing in $\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.\n(II) $f^{\\prime}$ is decreasing in $\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.<br />Between the above two statements,"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "only (II) is true.",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "only (I) is true.",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "neither (I) nor (II) is true.",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "both (I) and (II) are true",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x})=\\sin \\mathrm{x}+3 \\mathrm{x}-\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\left(\\mathrm{x}^2+\\mathrm{x}\\right) \\quad \\mathrm{x} \\in\\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right] \\\\ & \\mathrm{f}^{\\prime}(\\mathrm{x})=\\cos \\mathrm{x}+3-\\frac{2}{\\pi}(2 \\mathrm{x}+1)>0 \\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x}) \\uparrow \\\\ & \\mathrm{f}^{\\prime}(\\mathrm{x})=-\\sin \\mathrm{x}+0-\\frac{\\pi}{2}(2) \\\\ & =-\\sin \\mathrm{x}-\\frac{4}{\\pi} < 0 \\quad \\mathrm{f}^{\\prime}(\\mathrm{x}) \\downarrow \\\\ & 0 < \\mathrm{x} < \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}$\n$\\Rightarrow-\\frac{2}{\\pi}(\\underset{+1}{0}{ < \\underset{+1}2 \\mathrm{x}} < \\underset{+1}\\pi)$\n$\\begin{aligned} & \\underset{+3}{-\\frac{2}{\\pi}}>\\frac{-2}{\\pi}(\\underset{+3}2 \\mathrm{x}+1)>-\\frac{2}{\\pi}(\\underset{+3}\\pi+1) \\\\ &   \\underset{+ve} 3-\\frac{2}{\\pi}>3-\\frac{2}{\\pi}(2 x+1)>3-\\frac{2}{\\pi}(\\underset{+ve}\\pi+1) \\\\ & \\end{aligned}$"
  }
